package Patterns;

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Q2 {
    public static void main(String[] args){
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Please enter n: ");
        int n = input.nextInt();
        for (int i = 1; i <=n ; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <=i; j++) {
                System.out.print(j);

            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT:
n = 5
1
12
123
1234 
12345 

Approach
Take an integer n as input from the user.
Use an outer loop to iterate through the rows from 1 to n.
For each row i, use an inner loop to print numbers from 1 to i.
After printing all numbers for the current row, move to the next line.
Repeat the process until all rows are printed.

Logic
The outer loop controls the number of rows.
The inner loop prints numbers starting from 1 up to the current row number.
As the row number increases, one additional number is printed in each row, forming a triangular pattern.

Time Complexity
Outer loop runs n times.
Inner loop runs 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n times.

Total operations = n(n + 1) / 2

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity

Space Complexity: O(1)
